Cell[BoxData[
 InterpretationBox[Cell[BoxData[
   FormBox["\<\"### Report on the Work of Kevin Carlberg\\n\\nKevin \
Carlberg's research focuses on advancing reduced-order modeling (ROM) \
techniques to address computational challenges in high-fidelity simulations \
of parameterized systems. His contributions span a wide range of topics, \
including adaptive refinement, error quantification, nonlinear model \
reduction, and statistical modeling. Below is a summary of his key \
contributions based on the provided articles:\\n\\n---\\n\\n#### 1. \
**Adaptive Refinement for Reduced-Order Models**\\n   - **Key Work**: \
*Adaptive $h$-refinement for reduced-order models* (1404.0442)\\n   - \
**Contribution**: Developed an adaptive $h$-refinement method for ROMs that \
enriches the reduced basis online by splitting basis vectors using $k$-means \
clustering. This approach ensures error control and enables ROMs to capture \
phenomena like moving shocks, achieving equivalence to full-order models \
(FOMs) when fully refined.\\n\\n---\\n\\n#### 2. **Error Quantification and \
Statistical Modeling**\\n   - **Key Works**:\\n     - *The ROMES method for \
statistical modeling of reduced-order-model error* (1405.5170)\\n     - \
*Statistical closure modeling for reduced-order models of stationary systems \
by the ROMES method* (1901.02792)\\n   - **Contribution**: Introduced the \
ROMES method, which uses Gaussian-process regression to model ROM errors \
probabilistically. Extended this to construct statistical closure models for \
stationary systems, enabling efficient error quantification and propagation \
to quantities of interest. These methods improve ROM accuracy and robustness \
in uncertainty quantification (UQ) applications.\\n\\n---\\n\\n#### 3. \
**Comparative Analysis of Galerkin and LSPG Projections**\\n   - **Key \
Work**: *Galerkin v. least-squares Petrov--Galerkin projection in nonlinear \
model reduction* (1504.03749)\\n   - **Contribution**: Conducted a \
theoretical and computational comparison of Galerkin and Least-Squares Petrov\
\[Dash]Galerkin (LSPG) projections. Demonstrated that LSPG achieves lower \
error bounds and better stability for time-discrete systems, particularly in \
large-scale fluid dynamics problems.\\n\\n---\\n\\n#### 4. **Time-Parallelism \
and Data-Driven Forecasting**\\n   - **Key Work**: *Data-driven time \
parallelism via forecasting* (1610.09049)\\n   - **Contribution**: Proposed a \
data-driven approach to enable time-parallel numerical solutions for ODEs \
using gappy POD and forecasting techniques. This method enhances the \
scalability of ROMs by addressing temporal complexity and achieving \
near-ideal speedups in time-parallel frameworks.\\n\\n---\\n\\n#### 5. \
**Machine Learning for Error Modeling**\\n   - **Key Work**: *Error modeling \
for surrogates of dynamical systems using machine learning* (1701.03240)\\n   \
- **Contribution**: Developed a machine-learning-based framework (EMML) for \
error modeling in ROMs. By automating feature selection and using \
high-dimensional regression techniques, this approach improves error \
prediction and correction, particularly for nonlinear dynamical \
systems.\\n\\n---\\n\\n#### 6. **Space-Time Model Reduction**\\n   - **Key \
Work**: *Space-time least-squares Petrov-Galerkin projection for nonlinear \
model reduction* (1703.04560)\\n   - **Contribution**: Introduced a \
space-time LSPG method that simultaneously reduces spatial and temporal \
dimensions. This approach addresses temporal complexity, removes spurious \
modes, and achieves significant computational savings while maintaining \
accuracy.\\n\\n---\\n\\n#### 7. **Conservative Model Reduction**\\n   - **Key \
Work**: *Conservative model reduction for finite-volume models* \
(1711.11550)\\n   - **Contribution**: Developed conservative Galerkin and \
LSPG projection methods for finite-volume models, ensuring conservation \
properties and improving accuracy for fluid dynamics problems. This framework \
addresses the limitations of standard ROMs in preserving conservation \
laws.\\n\\n---\\n\\n#### 8. **Nonlinear Manifold Reduction with Deep \
Learning**\\n   - **Key Work**: *Model reduction of dynamical systems on \
nonlinear manifolds using deep convolutional autoencoders* (1812.08373)\\n   \
- **Contribution**: Proposed a novel framework for ROMs using nonlinear \
manifolds constructed with deep convolutional autoencoders. This method \
overcomes the limitations of linear subspaces, achieving superior accuracy \
for advection-dominated problems and systems with slowly decaying Kolmogorov \
$n$-width.\\n\\n---\\n\\n### Summary of Impact\\nKevin Carlberg's work has \
significantly advanced the field of reduced-order modeling by addressing key \
challenges in accuracy, efficiency, and scalability. His contributions span \
adaptive refinement, statistical error modeling, space-time reduction, and \
the integration of machine learning and deep learning into ROM frameworks. \
These innovations have broad applications in real-time control, uncertainty \
quantification, and large-scale simulations, particularly in fluid dynamics \
and other computationally intensive fields.\"\>", TextForm]], "InlineText",
   ExpressionUUID->"97cc0406-4b28-4363-be24-afc536ee2dd5"],
  Text["### Report on the Work of Kevin Carlberg\n\nKevin Carlberg's research \
focuses on advancing reduced-order modeling (ROM) techniques to address \
computational challenges in high-fidelity simulations of parameterized \
systems. His contributions span a wide range of topics, including adaptive \
refinement, error quantification, nonlinear model reduction, and statistical \
modeling. Below is a summary of his key contributions based on the provided \
articles:\n\n---\n\n#### 1. **Adaptive Refinement for Reduced-Order Models**\n\
   - **Key Work**: *Adaptive $h$-refinement for reduced-order models* \
(1404.0442)\n   - **Contribution**: Developed an adaptive $h$-refinement \
method for ROMs that enriches the reduced basis online by splitting basis \
vectors using $k$-means clustering. This approach ensures error control and \
enables ROMs to capture phenomena like moving shocks, achieving equivalence \
to full-order models (FOMs) when fully refined.\n\n---\n\n#### 2. **Error \
Quantification and Statistical Modeling**\n   - **Key Works**:\n     - *The \
ROMES method for statistical modeling of reduced-order-model error* \
(1405.5170)\n     - *Statistical closure modeling for reduced-order models of \
stationary systems by the ROMES method* (1901.02792)\n   - **Contribution**: \
Introduced the ROMES method, which uses Gaussian-process regression to model \
ROM errors probabilistically. Extended this to construct statistical closure \
models for stationary systems, enabling efficient error quantification and \
propagation to quantities of interest. These methods improve ROM accuracy and \
robustness in uncertainty quantification (UQ) applications.\n\n---\n\n#### 3. \
**Comparative Analysis of Galerkin and LSPG Projections**\n   - **Key Work**: \
*Galerkin v. least-squares Petrov--Galerkin projection in nonlinear model \
reduction* (1504.03749)\n   - **Contribution**: Conducted a theoretical and \
computational comparison of Galerkin and Least-Squares Petrov\[Dash]Galerkin \
(LSPG) projections. Demonstrated that LSPG achieves lower error bounds and \
better stability for time-discrete systems, particularly in large-scale fluid \
dynamics problems.\n\n---\n\n#### 4. **Time-Parallelism and Data-Driven \
Forecasting**\n   - **Key Work**: *Data-driven time parallelism via \
forecasting* (1610.09049)\n   - **Contribution**: Proposed a data-driven \
approach to enable time-parallel numerical solutions for ODEs using gappy POD \
and forecasting techniques. This method enhances the scalability of ROMs by \
addressing temporal complexity and achieving near-ideal speedups in \
time-parallel frameworks.\n\n---\n\n#### 5. **Machine Learning for Error \
Modeling**\n   - **Key Work**: *Error modeling for surrogates of dynamical \
systems using machine learning* (1701.03240)\n   - **Contribution**: \
Developed a machine-learning-based framework (EMML) for error modeling in \
ROMs. By automating feature selection and using high-dimensional regression \
techniques, this approach improves error prediction and correction, \
particularly for nonlinear dynamical systems.\n\n---\n\n#### 6. **Space-Time \
Model Reduction**\n   - **Key Work**: *Space-time least-squares \
Petrov-Galerkin projection for nonlinear model reduction* (1703.04560)\n   - \
**Contribution**: Introduced a space-time LSPG method that simultaneously \
reduces spatial and temporal dimensions. This approach addresses temporal \
complexity, removes spurious modes, and achieves significant computational \
savings while maintaining accuracy.\n\n---\n\n#### 7. **Conservative Model \
Reduction**\n   - **Key Work**: *Conservative model reduction for \
finite-volume models* (1711.11550)\n   - **Contribution**: Developed \
conservative Galerkin and LSPG projection methods for finite-volume models, \
ensuring conservation properties and improving accuracy for fluid dynamics \
problems. This framework addresses the limitations of standard ROMs in \
preserving conservation laws.\n\n---\n\n#### 8. **Nonlinear Manifold \
Reduction with Deep Learning**\n   - **Key Work**: *Model reduction of \
dynamical systems on nonlinear manifolds using deep convolutional \
autoencoders* (1812.08373)\n   - **Contribution**: Proposed a novel framework \
for ROMs using nonlinear manifolds constructed with deep convolutional \
autoencoders. This method overcomes the limitations of linear subspaces, \
achieving superior accuracy for advection-dominated problems and systems with \
slowly decaying Kolmogorov $n$-width.\n\n---\n\n### Summary of Impact\nKevin \
Carlberg's work has significantly advanced the field of reduced-order \
modeling by addressing key challenges in accuracy, efficiency, and \
scalability. His contributions span adaptive refinement, statistical error \
modeling, space-time reduction, and the integration of machine learning and \
deep learning into ROM frameworks. These innovations have broad applications \
in real-time control, uncertainty quantification, and large-scale \
simulations, particularly in fluid dynamics and other computationally \
intensive fields."]]], "Output",
 CellChangeTimes->{3.9373005260194397`*^9, 3.942667176730739*^9},
 CellLabel->"Out[857]=",
 CellID->1978978533,ExpressionUUID->"fbc24d91-2b8e-4f81-9e65-f6c6a4b81bb0"]
}, Open  ]]
}, Open  ]]
